### What are the five use cases of flatMap?

### Use case 1: Flatten nested collections

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Collection;
import java.util.List;

public class FlattenDemo {
    record Branch(String name, List<String> accountIds) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Branch> branches = List.of(
            new Branch("Mumbai", List.of("SAV2001", "SAV2002")),
            new Branch("Delhi", List.of("CUR3001")),
            new Branch("Chennai", List.of("SAV2003", "FD4001", "CUR3002"))
        );

        List<String> allAccounts = branches.stream()
            .flatMap(b -> b.accountIds().stream())
            .toList();

        System.out.println("All accounts: " + allAccounts);
    }
}
```

```text
All accounts: [SAV2001, SAV2002, CUR3001, SAV2003, FD4001, CUR3002]
```

### Use case 2: Split one value into many

```java
List<String> allSymbols = List.of("RELIANCE,TCS,HDFC", "INFY,WIPRO").stream()
    .flatMap(csv -> java.util.Arrays.stream(csv.split(",")))
    .distinct()
    .toList();
// [RELIANCE, TCS, HDFC, INFY, WIPRO]
```

### Use case 3: Extract Optional values (Java 9)

```java
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.List;

List<String> emails = List.of(
    Optional.of("rajesh@bank.com"), Optional.empty(), Optional.of("amit@bank.com")
).stream()
    .flatMap(Optional::stream)  // present -> value, empty -> gone
    .toList();
// [rajesh@bank.com, amit@bank.com]
```

### Use case 4: Merge multiple stream sources

```java
import java.util.stream.Stream;

Stream<String> merged = Stream.of(
    List.of("SAV2001", "SAV2002").stream(),
    List.of("CUR3001", "CUR3002").stream()
).flatMap(s -> s);
// Stream<String> with all 4 accounts
```

### Use case 5: Multi-level drill-down

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;

public class MultiLevelDrillDown {
    record Customer(String name, List<Account> accounts) {}
    record Account(String id, List<Transaction> transactions) {}
    record Transaction(String txnId, String tag) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Customer> customers = List.of(
            new Customer("Rajesh", List.of(
                new Account("SAV2001", List.of(
                    new Transaction("T1", "TRANSFER"),
                    new Transaction("T2", "DEPOSIT")
                ))
            )),
            new Customer("Priya", List.of(
                new Account("SAV2002", List.of(
                    new Transaction("T3", "WITHDRAWAL")
                ))
            ))
        );

        // 3-level drill-down with chained flatMap
        List<String> allTags = customers.stream()
            .flatMap(c -> c.accounts().stream())
            .flatMap(a -> a.transactions().stream())
            .map(Transaction::tag)
            .distinct()
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Tags: " + allTags);
    }
}
```

```text
Tags: [TRANSFER, DEPOSIT, WITHDRAWAL]
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

flatMap is the monadic bind. It applies to both Stream and Optional. Whenever a mapping function returns a container type (Stream, Optional, List), flatMap unwraps and merges. Without flatMap, the equivalent code is nested for-loops or filter+map+isPresent chains.
